// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-11-17 (Tuesday) — Carnatic melakarta math
// Melakarta #46: Shadvidhamargini
// ============================================================================
//
// THE NUMBERING SYSTEM (same chakra/position scheme every Tuesday this
// project has used — 08-25's #22 through 11-10's #64):
//   chakra = ceil(N / 6), position = N - 6*(chakra - 1)
// For N = 46: chakra = ceil(46/6) = 8 ("Vasu" chakra) -> chakra > 6 means
// PRATI Ma (M2). Ri-Ga combo index = ((chakra-1) mod 6)+1 = ((7) mod 6)+1
// = 2 -> R1 G2 (shuddha Ri, sadharana Ga). position = 46 - 6*7 = 4 -> Dha-Ni
// combo #4 of its own 6-combo cycle (1=D1N1 2=D1N2 3=D1N3 4=D2N2 5=D2N3
// 6=D3N3) -> D2 N2 (chatushruti Dha, kaisiki Ni).
// melakarta 46 = S R1 G2 M2 P D2 N2. Semitones (S=0 R1=1 R2/G1=2 R3/G2=3
// G3=4 M1=5 M2=6 P=7 D1=8 D2/N1=9 D3/N2=10 N3=11): 0 1 3 6 7 9 10 — confirmed
// against Wikipedia's Shadvidamargini entry (ascent S R1 G2 M2 P D2 N2 Ṡ) and
// karnatik.com's mela table, independent of the chakra/position math above.
//
// WESTERN SCALE MATCH — checked this session with `npm install
// @tonaljs/scale-type` and scanned all 92 of its built-in 7-note scales for
// an exact chroma match to [0,1,3,6,7,9,10]: NONE MATCH (closest are
// distance-2 near-misses — dorian b2, dorian #4, hungarian major — none
// exact). Unlike most prior melakarta Tuesdays (08-25 Kharaharapriya/dorian,
// 09-29 Keeravani/harmonic minor, 10-06 Kalyani/lydian, 10-27 Harikambhoji/
// mixolydian, 11-03 Hanumatodi/phrygian, 11-10 Vachaspati/lydian dominant),
// this one has no clean 7-tone Western-mode name — same situation 10-20's
// Dhenuka hit (landed on a Balinese-pelog-adjacent set instead). Per the
// standing rule for exactly this case (CLAUDE.md's Monday-thaat fallback,
// applied here to a Tuesday number instead): don't guess a "close enough"
// name — map scale degrees to semitones explicitly instead (MELAKARTA_46 /
// toMidi below), same discipline as the explicit-notation fallback.
// The actual character: a 1-2-3-1-2-1-2 semitone gap pattern — in
// particular a 3-semitone gap between G2 and M2 (adjacent SCALE degrees a
// minor third apart in pitch) where every named mode above has only 1 or 2.
// That gap is this raga's whole identity and drives the melody below.
//
// KATAPAYADI CROSS-CHECK: first two consonants of "SHAdVIdhamargini" are
// "sha" and the conjunct "dvi" (counts only its LAST consonant, "va", per
// the katapayadi conjunct rule). Using this project's own consonant table
// (established in 11-03's and 11-10's files): sha -> row 6 (ca/ta/sha),
// va -> row 4 (gha/dha/bha/va). Digits in name order (6, 4) -> REVERSE to
// get the melakarta number: 46. Matches the chakra/position math above
// independently, and matches chakra 8's "Vasu" family (43 Gavambhodi,
// 44 Bhavapriya, 45 Shubhapantuvarali, 46 Shadvidhamargini is the 4th of six
// — position 4, confirmed a third way).
//
// THE MATH DRIVING TODAY'S PATTERN — four distinct derived numbers, each
// with its own audible job:
//   - N's own two digits (4, 6) -> phrase lengths. Call = 4 steps, response
//     = 6 steps: the melakarta number itself generates the melody's
//     structure, not an arbitrary substitute (the point of this slot).
//   - chakra (8) -> lead filter-sweep LFO period, 8 cycles — chakra 8 is
//     exactly what makes this prati Ma (the sharp 4th causing the
//     exotic G-M gap), so the number that causes the color drives the
//     parameter that lets it keep moving.
//   - position (4) -> lead delay time, a quarter-note subdivision (1/4).
//   - Ri-Ga combo index (2) -> the drone dyad's alternation period, 2
//     cycles (see ARRANGEMENT below).
//
// ARRANGEMENT — compared directly against 11-10's Vachaspati (the most
// recent prior Tuesday, also prati Ma): that file used a 3-voice consonant
// harmonic stack drone, a square lead, and a pan LFO for its number-driven
// accent. Today changes all three, driven by what's actually distinctive
// about THIS melakarta — the unresolved G-M gap, not a consonant overtone
// color:
//   drone:  a 2-note dyad, tonic + the sharp-4 color tone (M2) — a true
//           tritone against the tonic, left to ring rather than resolved —
//           ALTERNATING every 2 cycles (Ri-Ga combo index) with tonic + Pa
//           (a plain fifth), so the drone itself states tension then
//           release, the same device the melody uses, instead of sitting
//           on one static interval all the way through. Still plain sine
//           (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5), still slow attack/release.
//   lead:   triangle (synth-basic) — not square (11-10) or sawtooth
//           (11-03): triangle's harmonic content is the softest, least
//           buzzy of the three, chosen because this scale's own color
//           already supplies the tension (the G-M gap) — a harsher
//           waveform on top of it would double up on edge rather than
//           let the interval itself be heard clearly.
//   accent: filter sweep (chakra) + delay time (position) on the lead
//           itself, not a separate plucked layer or pan LFO — a different
//           device from 11-10's pan automation.
//   No percussion: a Carnatic alapana-style exposition over a drone has no
//   metric kit (MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md's autopilot-percussion lesson, and the
//   standing rule every melakarta Tuesday so far has kept).
//
// PITCH MAPPING — no .scale() name matches (see WESTERN SCALE MATCH above),
// so scale degrees map to MIDI explicitly: MELAKARTA_46 holds the raga's own
// semitone offsets, toMidi(d) wraps octaves and looks the degree up,
// .withValue(toMidi) rewrites the degree-number pattern into real pitches —
// the same raga-mapper technique MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §13/§20 documents for
// the songs lane (seq + withValue), used here because this is exactly that
// situation: a real scale with no named Western equivalent to borrow.
//
// MELODY — four phrases (call / full descending run-with-resolution / drop-
// with-anticipation / motif-variation), anchored on S (tonic), built to make
// the G-M gap audible as a "step" rather than hide it:
//   A (call, 4 steps — N's tens digit): tonic, rest, leap a fifth to Pa,
//     leap a further minor third up into the flat-7 tension tone, left
//     hanging:
//                                          0 ~ 4 6
//   B (full descending run, 6 steps — N's ones digit; call-and-response,
//     answering A's hang by resuming from the same note A ended on): N
//     D P M -then the G-M gap itself, scale-degree-adjacent (M->G) but a
//     minor third in pitch- G, resolving down to S:
//                                          6 5 4 3 2 0
//   C (the drop, 6 steps; tension->release/anticipation device): octave-up
//     tonic leapt down a tritone onto the color tone (M), rest, then a
//     further big leap down onto the approach-tone from below (degree -1,
//     the flat-7 of the octave below) — left hanging, unresolved:
//                                          7 ~ 3 ~ -1 ~
//   D (motif-and-variation of A, 4 steps; resolves C's hang): the same
//     opening fifth-leap-then-minor-third as A, but closed with a near-
//     octave leap straight back down to the tonic instead of hanging:
//                                          0 4 6 0
//
// CYCLES = 20. setcpm(60) -> 20 * 60/60 = 20.0s, five clean passes of the
// 4-phrase rotation (A/B/C/D). No tala implied (no drum layer to check a
// genre BPM against) — cpm here just paces the phrase slots for legibility,
// same reasoning prior melakarta Tuesdays used.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(60)
let CYCLES = 20 // 20 * 60/60 = 20.0s; five clean passes of the 4-phrase melody

// Melakarta 46 (Shadvidhamargini): S R1 G2 M2 P D2 N2 — semitones from tonic.
// No tonaljs scale matches this set (see WESTERN SCALE MATCH above), so
// degrees map to MIDI explicitly instead of through .scale().
const MELAKARTA_46 = [0, 1, 3, 6, 7, 9, 10]
const TONIC = 66 // F#4 — unused as a Tuesday tonic so far (C, D, E, F, G, A, B, Bb already used)
const toMidi = (d) => {
  const oct = Math.floor(d / 7)
  const idx = ((d % 7) + 7) % 7
  return TONIC + 12 * oct + MELAKARTA_46[idx]
}

stack(
  // DRONE — tonic + the raga's own color tone (M2, a tritone against the
  // tonic), alternating every 2 cycles (Ri-Ga combo index) with tonic + Pa
  // (a plain fifth) — see ARRANGEMENT above for why this replaces last
  // week's static 3-voice stack. f#2 = TONIC-24, c3 = +M2, cs3 = +P.
  note("<[f#2,c3] [f#2,cs3]>/2")
    .sound("sine")
    .gain(.26)
    .attack(2.6).release(3.3)
    .room(4.5),

  // MELODY — see MELODY note above. Real leaps (a fifth, minor thirds, a
  // tritone, a near-octave drop, an approach from below), rests,
  // call-and-response (A/B), tension->release (C/D), and motif-and-
  // variation (D vs A) as the required jazz-improv devices.
  note(`<
    0 ~ 4 6
    6 5 4 3 2 0
    7 ~ 3 ~ -1 ~
    0 4 6 0
  >`)
    .withValue(toMidi)
    .sound("triangle")
    .attack(.01).decay(.15).sustain(.12).release(.25)
    .lpf(sine.range(380, 2200).slow(8)) // chakra (8) -> sweep period
    .lpq(5)
    .rarely(x => x.add(note(12))) // improv hook: occasional octave leap
    .gain(.44)
    .room(2.3)
    .delay(.2)
    .delaytime(1/4) // position (4) -> quarter-note delay subdivision
    .delayfeedback(.22)
)
